  "prompt": "[system]\nYou generate executable, reliable VRP metaheuristics for evolutionary search.\n\n[user]\nSTRATEGY m3_simplify_generalize: Create a simpler and more robust version of one parent by removing brittle, over-specialized, redundant, or overly complex components. Preserve the essential idea and required behavior while improving generalization and maintainability.\n\nUse the provided parents as the primary source of design changes.\n\nIncorporate, adapt, recombine, repair, or refine the listed parents.\n\nDo not ignore the parents or generate an unrelated solver from scratch.\n\nVRP objective: lower maximum route distance across trucks is better. Feasibility is mandatory.\n\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000001: savings_construction_improvement\nStatus: valid; gap: 38.2348\nThought: Build routes using Clarke-Wright savings: start with each customer as a separate route, then iteratively merge routes with the largest positive savings until truck_count is reached. After construction, apply local search (relocate and swap) to minimize the maximum route distance. Tie-breaking is deterministic by customer index.\nMean gap: 38.23484955387433\nMean max route distance: 2.828261022584995\nMean total route distance: 16.88274244935178\nGap by instance size: {'100': 38.23484955387433}\nGap by truck count: {'6': 38.23484955387433}\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: vrp:size:100\nHistory role: regime specialist\nCode:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef solve_vrp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray, truck_count: int) -> list[list[int]]:\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    if n == 1:\n        return [[0, 0] for _ in range(truck_count)]\n    customers = list(range(1, n))\n    num_customers = len(customers)\n    if truck_count >= num_customers:\n        routes = [[0, c, 0] for c in customers]\n        for _ in range(truck_count - num_customers):\n            routes.append([0, 0])\n        return routes\n    # Initialize each customer as a route (list of customers, no depot)\n    route_list = [[c] for c in customers]\n    # Map customer to route index\n    customer_to_route = {c: idx for idx, c in enumerate(customers)}\n    # Track first and last customer of each route\n    route_first = {idx: c for idx, c in enumerate(customers)}\n    route_last = {idx: c for idx, c in enumerate(customers)}\n    current_route_count = num_customers\n    # Compute savings\n    savings = []\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            s = distance_matrix[0][i] + distance_matrix[0][j] - distance_matrix[i][j]\n            if s > 0:\n                savings.append((s, i, j))\n    savings.sort(key=lambda x: -x[0])\n    # Merge routes\n    for s, i, j in savings:\n        if current_route_count <= truck_count:\n            break\n        if customer_to_route[i] == customer_to_route[j]:\n            continue\n        ri = customer_to_route[i]\n        rj = customer_to_route[j]\n        route_a = route_list[ri]\n        route_b = route_list[rj]\n        merged_route = None\n        for a in (route_a, route_a[::-1]):\n            for b in (route_b, route_b[::-1]):\n                if a[-1] == i and b[0] == j:\n                    merged_route = a + b\n                    break\n                elif a[-1] == j and b[0] == i:\n                    merged_route = a + b\n                    break\n            if merged_route is not None:\n                break\n        if merged_route is None:\n            continue\n        # Replace ri with merged, remove rj\n        route_list[ri] = merged_route\n        route_list.pop(rj)\n        # Update mappings\n        for idx, cust in enumerate(merged_route):\n            customer_to_route[cust] = ri\n        route_first[ri] = merged_route[0]\n        route_last[ri] = merged_route[-1]\n        # Remove entries for rj, shift larger indices down\n        del route_first[rj]\n        del route_last[rj]\n        # Adjust indices for routes after rj\n        for key in list(customer_to_route.keys()):\n            if customer_to_route[key] > rj:\n                customer_to_route[key] -= 1\n        for key in list(route_first.keys()):\n            if key > rj:\n                route_first[key-1] = route_first.pop(key)\n                route_last[key-1] = route_last.pop(key)\n        current_route_count -= 1\n    # Fallback: if still too many routes, merge arbitrarily\n    while current_route_count > truck_count:\n        # Merge first two routes\n        ri = 0\n        rj = 1\n        route_a = route_list[ri]\n        route_b = route_list[rj]\n        merged_route = route_a + route_b\n        route_list[ri] = merged_route\n        route_list.pop(rj)\n        for cust in merged_route:\n            customer_to_route[cust] = ri\n        route_first[ri] = merged_route[0]\n        route_last[ri] = merged_route[-1]\n        del route_first[rj]\n        del route_last[rj]\n        # Adjust indices\n        for key in list(customer_to_route.keys()):\n            if customer_to_route[key] > rj:\n                customer_to_route[key] -= 1\n        for key in list(route_first.keys()):\n            if key > rj:\n                route_first[key-1] = route_first.pop(key)\n                route_last[key-1] = route_last.pop(key)\n        current_route_count -= 1\n    # Build full routes with depot\n    full_routes = [[0] + r + [0] for r in route_list]\n    best_routes = [r[:] for r in full_routes]\n    best_max = max(sum(distance_matrix[r[i]][r[i+1]] for i in range(len(r)-1)) for r in full_routes)\n    # Local search: relocate and swap\n    max_iters = 10 * num_customers\n    for _ in range(max_iters):\n        improved = False\n        # Try relocate each customer to another route\n        for cust in customers:\n            src_route_idx = None\n            src_pos = None\n            for idx, r in enumerate(full_routes):\n                if cust in r:\n                    src_route_idx = idx\n                    src_pos = r.index(cust)\n                    break\n            if src_route_idx is None:\n                continue\n            src_route = full_routes[src_route_idx]\n            # Remove cust from src\n            temp_src = src_route[:]\n            temp_src.pop(src_pos)\n            # For each destination route, including same? we'll consider all different\n            for dst_idx in range(len(full_routes)):\n                if dst_idx == src_route_idx:\n                    continue\n                dst_route = full_routes[dst_idx]\n                # Try inserting cust at each position (after depot, before last depot)\n                for pos in range(1, len(dst_route)):  # insert before pos? actually insert at pos means after depots? we need to ensure start/end depot\n                    # Better: new_dst = dst_route[:pos] + [cust] + dst_route[pos:]\n                    new_dst = dst_route[:pos] + [cust] + dst_route[pos:]\n                    # Ensure first and last are 0?\n                    if new_dst[0] != 0 or new_dst[-1] != 0:\n                        continue\n                    # Compute new max\n                    new_routes = [r[:] for r in full_routes]\n                    new_routes[src_route_idx] = temp_src\n                    new_routes[dst_idx] = new_dst\n                    new_max = max(sum(distance_matrix[r[i]][r[i+1]] for i in range(len(r)-1)) for r in new_routes)\n                    if new_max < best_max:\n                        best_max = new_max\n                        best_routes = [r[:] for r in new_routes]\n                        improved = True\n            if improved:\n                break\n        if improved:\n            full_routes = [r[:] for r in best_routes]\n            continue\n        # Try swap two customers\n        for cust1 in customers:\n            for cust2 in customers:\n                if cust1 >= cust2:\n                    continue\n                # Find routes\n                r1_idx = r2_idx = None\n                for idx, r in enumerate(full_routes):\n                    if cust1 in r:\n                        r1_idx = idx\n                    if cust2 in r:\n                        r2_idx = idx\n                if r1_idx is None or r2_idx is None:\n                    continue\n                if r1_idx == r2_idx:\n                    continue\n                r1 = full_routes[r1_idx]\n                r2 = full_routes[r2_idx]\n                p1 = r1.index(cust1)\n                p2 = r2.index(cust2)\n                new_r1 = r1[:]\n                new_r2 = r2[:]\n                new_r1[p1] = cust2\n                new_r2[p2] = cust1\n                new_routes = [r[:] for r in full_routes]\n                new_routes[r1_idx] = new_r1\n                new_routes[r2_idx] = new_r2\n                new_max = max(sum(distance_matrix[r[i]][r[i+1]] for i in range(len(r)-1)) for r in new_routes)\n                if new_max < best_max:\n                    best_max = new_max\n                    best_routes = [r[:] for r in new_routes]\n                    improved = True\n                    break\n            if improved:\n                break\n        if not improved:\n            break\n        full_routes = [r[:] for r in best_routes]\n    # Ensure exactly truck_count routes\n    while len(best_routes) > truck_count:\n        # Merge two shortest routes\n        dists = []\n        for idx, r in enumerate(best_routes):\n            d = sum(distance_matrix[r[i]][r[i+1]] for i in range(len(r)-1))\n            dists.append((d, idx))\n        dists.sort()\n        i = dists[0][1]\n        j = dists[1][1]\n        new_route = best_routes[i][:-1] + best_routes[j][1:]\n        best_routes[i] = new_route\n        best_routes.pop(j)\n    while len(best_routes) < truck_count:\n        best_routes.append([0,0])\n    return best_routes\n```\n\nImplement exactly this interface:\n\ndef solve_vrp(\n    distance_matrix: np.ndarray,  # (n, n), depot is node 0\n    truck_count: int,\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n\nRules:\n- VRP is a static multi-truck routing problem; the objective is to minimize the maximum route distance across trucks.\n- Return exactly truck_count routes.\n- Each route must start at depot node 0 and end at depot node 0.\n- Every customer node 1..n-1 must appear exactly once across all routes.\n- An unused truck route must be [0, 0].\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates, instance size, truck count, or dataset details.\n- Keep all search and improvement loops finite and bounded by instance size; never use open-ended loops.\n- Call report_best_vrp(routes) whenever you find a better complete feasible route set, especially before expensive improvement loops.\n- Do not read/write files, use network, spawn subprocesses, or call external solvers.\n- Allowed imports only: numpy, math, random, heapq, itertools, collections, time.\n- No module-level mutable globals; the solver may be called many times across instances.\n\nInternal check before final JSON: correct solve_vrp signature, exactly truck_count routes,\nevery route starts and ends at 0, every customer appears exactly once, empty trucks use [0, 0],\nfinite instance-size-bounded loops, deterministic tie handling, report_best_vrp used for\nincumbents, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short snake_case_or_title name\",\n  \"thought\": \"brief public summary of the construction, balancing, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\n\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_vrp.",
